body:
Window callbacks are registered here:
```cpp
void Window::_update_window_callbacks() {
	DisplayServer::get_singleton()->window_set_rect_changed_callback(callable_mp(this, &Window::_rect_changed_callback), window_id);
	DisplayServer::get_singleton()->window_set_window_event_callback(callable_mp(this, &Window::_event_callback), window_id);
	DisplayServer::get_singleton()->window_set_input_event_callback(callable_mp(this, &Window::_window_input), window_id);
	DisplayServer::get_singleton()->window_set_input_text_callback(callable_mp(this, &Window::_window_input_text), window_id);
	DisplayServer::get_singleton()->window_set_drop_files_callback(callable_mp(this, &Window::_window_drop_files), window_id);
}
```

On Android the implementation doesn't seem to care about `window_id`, but I suppose we only support a single window anyway:
```cpp
void DisplayServerAndroid::window_set_window_event_callback(const Callable &p_callable, DisplayServer::WindowID p_window) {
	window_event_callback = p_callable;
}

void DisplayServerAndroid::window_set_input_event_callback(const Callable &p_callable, DisplayServer::WindowID p_window) {
	input_event_callback = p_callable;
}

void DisplayServerAndroid::window_set_input_text_callback(const Callable &p_callable, DisplayServer::WindowID p_window) {
	input_text_callback = p_callable;
}

void DisplayServerAndroid::window_set_rect_changed_callback(const Callable &p_callable, DisplayServer::WindowID p_window) {
	rect_changed_callback = p_callable;
}

void DisplayServerAndroid::window_set_drop_files_callback(const Callable &p_callable, DisplayServer::WindowID p_window) {
	// Not supported on Android.
}
```

But so should `~Window` be responsible for unregistering those callbacks from `DisplayServer`?

*Edit:* Even in single window mode, `Popup`s and `*Dialog`s are `Window`s too. Does that mean that any time a Popup is created on Android, it will replace the callbacks in DisplayServerAndroid, and since it doesn't clean after itself we leave things in a broken state?

> _Edit:_ Even in single window mode, `Popup`s and `*Dialog`s are `Window`s too. Does that mean that any time a Popup is created on Android, it will replace the callbacks in DisplayServerAndroid, and since it doesn't clean after itself we leave things in a broken state?

Tested this hypothesis a bit with a project that creates a `Window` at runtime, as well as using an `OptionButton`, but I couldn't trigger an issue. In fact `Window::_update_window_callbacks` didn't seem to run for either of those secondary windows, and looking at `window.cpp` some more, it seems like `Window::_update_window_callbacks` would only be called for native windows (not embedded). Android doesn't seem to support native windows, so that explains why it only cares about a single window ID (root viewport).

